{
  "case_id": "2016-15-680",
  "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Appellants",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The district court created out of whole cloth a new legal standard that permitted Virginia to apply a one-size-fits-all, 55 percent racial floor to all 12 of its predominantly black districts. Virginia applied this 55 percent rule to move voters in and move voters out of districts on the basis of race, regardless of the differences in voting patterns, geography, demographics, or the actual interests of black voters in each of those districts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: I'm -- I'm not quite sure I understand how you assess predominance, which I think is the challenge here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What if the legislature says, look, we want to follow all the traditional districting, applying all the traditional districting factors. However, one thing we absolutely do not want is to be held to have violated Section 5 or Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act. So we have these 12 majority-African-American districts, and we don't want to do anything to them that results in liability under the Voting Rights Act. Is that predominance?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Appellants",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ELIAS: His mistake is setting an arbitrary threshold at 55 percent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: But -- but as to 75, did he not say that strict scrutiny was met, because other legitimate and -- and conventional factors were considered and were present?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Elias",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "look at what the actual evidence was to meet the strong base of evidence -- because once -- once we found -- once predominance was found and strict scrutiny applied, now the burden shifted to the government to explain why they had a strong basis of evidence in doing what they did."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Irving L. Gornstein",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0001-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The district court was right to hold that the use of a racial target is not sufficient to trigger strict scrutiny, but it was wrong to hold that a conflict with traditional redistricting principles is an essential element of a racial gerrymandering claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Now, I have a particular question on 71. Remember what I was trying do."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: Right. JUSTICE BREYER: Now, let's look at 71. Same kind of thing they are arguing. Same kind of thing. They moved -- I don't know, you have it in the SG's brief too. They moved 11,293 people out and 17,000 in. So let's look at those people. The ones they moved out were three-quarters or something white, and the ones they moved in were three-quarters or something black. So that's pretty similar. It seems to me they paid a lot of attention to race."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0002-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "He looked at the district as drawn. The first thing he noticed is that it preserves 78 percent of the core of the district, which is higher than the statewide average of 70 percent. So you have the core of the district is being preserved, which is a traditional districting principle."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, it's more than a theoretical possibility. And Mr. Gornstein says -- and he seems to be pretty sensitive to the idea of giving States latitude. But he looks at this and says, this standard actually did make a difference on the ground, that there were districts kicked out and said, oh, this isn't race-based because it looks good, even though it was race-based."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State Legislature of Virginia",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "representing the State Legislature of Virginia, my bona fides in looking out for the State's even ahead of Mr. Gornstein's. And it's easy in the Solicitor General's Office to throw out a standard that's theoretically pure and that's going to force lots of other people to litigate for years."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Appellants",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ELIAS: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: I want to clarify a few factual points and obviously answer any questions you have."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2016-15-680",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "15706af82df1d98e0e77c51487f29d9fe09fa84db08fc1d7b0abf74921de1762",
      "evidence_id": "ev-15706af82df1-0003-0003",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Stevens argues that strict scrutiny does not apply where the State respects or complies with traditional districting principles."
    },
    {
